# VALIDATOR WORKFLOW

- The agent workspace does not own the Docker daemon. Use `cage-check` to ask the host-side validator to build and boot what you have written so far.
- Do not spend many rounds polishing before the first validator call. As soon as `docker-compose.yml`, `build_report.json`, and `challenge.json` exist in minimally valid form, run `cage-check`.
- Run `cage-check` after each meaningful Compose or Dockerfile change. A failed check returns a structured failure class plus concrete feedback; fix that exact issue before moving on.
- `cage-check` itself is the independent validator. It resolves the private target address and executes the structured `runtime_probes` from `challenge.json` on the host; agent-authored `service_output.txt` is never grading truth.
- On an R4 `PASS`, quote or summarize the returned host-side probe excerpt in your final response, run `cage-check stop`, and finish. Do not repeat the check, manually replace it with `nc -vz`, or continue redesigning a target that already passed.
- On an R3-only `PASS`, do not call the result R4. A port-open or process-liveness observation cannot promote the environment.
- If feedback has `retryable: false`, stop blind repair attempts. Preserve the structured blocker, run `cage-check stop` if needed, and finish.

